From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 12:02:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] Link a 2 MiB stack and allow a 1 MiB stack limit

The binary was linked with a 1 MiB stack and MAX_STACK_SIZE reserved
half of it, so no VM could recurse deeper than 512 KiB of stack. Worse,
the engine's default limit (JS_DEFAULT_STACK_SIZE, 1 MiB) is ignored
when the stack is not larger than it, so a VM created without
maxStackSize had no stack check at all: deep recursion ran off the
bottom of the stack or overflowed the host's native stack, and in both
cases the call failed outside the VM and left it unusable.

The stack is now 2 MiB and MAX_STACK_SIZE 1 MiB. A VM gets
DEFAULT_STACK_SIZE (512 KiB) when maxStackSize is not given: the host
engine runs the WASM code on its own native stack, which recursion
fills a little faster than the WASM stack, and 512 KiB is what fits
before a 1 MiB native stack (Node.js's main thread) overflows, for
every recursion pattern the tests try. 1 MiB fits a native stack of
about 2 MiB or more (JavaScriptCore, a Node.js worker). A restored VM
keeps the limit of its snapshot unless given one.

The VMs of an instance share its stack, and a runtime measures its
limit from where it was created, so a VM created deep in the stack (from
the host function of another VM) could get a limit below the bottom of
the stack. interface.c keeps every VM's limit at least 64 KiB above the
bottom of the stack (__stack_low); a test that creates a VM at the
bottom of another VM's recursion trapped with "memory access out of
bounds" without it.

The new tests check that each recursion pattern stops with "Maximum
call stack size exceeded" and leaves the VM usable, at the default on
the test's own thread, at 1 MiB in a worker with a 4 MiB stack (which
loads the built package from dist/), and from nested VMs.
